      toast.js         transient banner for events that fire while you're looking
    ui/
      task-list.js     task rendering and input handling
  test/
    storage.test.js  tasks.test.js  scheduler.test.js  notifications.test.js

  Dependency direction is one-way: [38;5;153mui/[39m and [38;5;153mchannels/[39m depend on the stores; the stores depend on [38;5;153mstorage.js[39m; [38;5;153mscheduler.js
[39m  reads tasks and emits to the bus; nothing under [38;5;153msrc/[39m outside [38;5;153mui/[39m and [38;5;153mchannels/[39m touches [38;5;153mdocument[39m. That's what makes
  the core testable in Node with no jsdom.

  The two seams that matter, because they're the ones a real backend would later plug into:

  - [1m[38;5;153mEventSource[0m — [38;5;153msubscribe(handler) → unsubscribe[39m. [38;5;153mscheduler.js[39m is one implementation. A future WebSocket stream is
    another, and nothing downstream changes.
  - [1m[38;5;153mChannel[0m — [38;5;153mdeliver(notification)[39m. [38;5;153mcentre[39m and [38;5;153mtoast[39m implement it. An OS-notification channel is a drop-in third.

  [38;5;153mscheduler.js[39m never writes to the notification store directly — it emits onto the bus, and a single subscriber in
  [38;5;153mmain.js[39m fans events out to channels and appends to the store. That keeps "what happened" separate from "who gets
  told," which is the distinction that makes this a notifications system rather than a pile of timers.

